Make secure DNS work where Chromium quietly gives up on it, and stop DoH
queries carrying headers they have no business carrying.

Requested as android issue 17, which asks for two Cromite patches by name:
DoH-improvements.patch and
Reduce-HTTP-headers-in-DoH-requests-to-bare-minimum.patch. This takes the parts
of them that are defensible and leaves the parts that are not, so it is a port
rather than a copy.

WHAT IS TAKEN, and why each one.

1. Minimal headers on DoH queries, which is the whole of the second patch. A
   DoH query is an HTTP POST carrying wire-format DNS, and Chromium sends it
   with a Referer, a User-Agent, Accept-Encoding advertising Brotli and Zstd,
   and an Accept-Language. RFC 8484 section 6.1 says implementations SHOULD NOT
   set non-essential headers in DoH requests, and the reason is exactly what it
   sounds like: every one of those is a stable identifier handed to the
   resolver on every lookup, and the resolver is the party you moved to DoH to
   stop trusting. A new LOAD_MINIMAL_HEADERS flag, set only by DnsHTTPAttempt,
   suppresses all four.

2. An explicit DoH server plus a secure mode is now enough to build a DNS
   config. Upstream requires DnsConfigOverrides::OverridesEverything(), so if
   the system DNS configuration is missing or unreadable, BuildEffectiveConfig
   returns nullopt and the resolver falls back - silently - to the platform
   resolver, ignoring the DoH server the user chose. On Android that is not an
   edge case: a VPN or Private DNS in the path is routine. This is the half of
   the request that is a real bug fix rather than a preference.

3. Secure DNS is no longer switched off because the device merely looks managed
   or parentally filtered. Chromium disables DoH when it detects an MDM or
   device-owner app, or a parental-control filter, on the reasoning that those
   exist to see and filter DNS. That does not survive contact with a browser
   people install to stop exactly that: a work profile on the same phone should
   not silently revoke a setting the user chose for their own browsing.

   This is the part an earlier version of this patch took from Cromite, wrote
   the way Cromite writes it - `if ((true)) return false;` at the top of three
   functions, original bodies left behind - and then removed again, because
   Chromium 152 compiles with -Wunreachable-code-aggressive (line 2173 of
   build/config/compiler/BUILD.gn) and warnings are errors. It is back, done
   differently: the detection functions are untouched, and the two call sites
   that actually turn DoH off are gated on a base::Feature. A feature keeps each
   condition a runtime value, so nothing is provably unreachable, and it leaves
   a way back for anyone who does want the platform's answer to win.

   Only the two call sites that disable DoH are gated. The third use of
   ShouldDisableDohForManaged() is inside `if (record_metrics)` and only picks a
   histogram, so it still reports the truth about the device, and the
   parental-controls delay timer is left alone because all it does is trigger a
   config refresh.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN.

Cromite's DoH-improvements.patch also rewrites SecureDnsConfig::ParseMode so
that "automatic" means "secure". That is not a small change of default, it is a
change of failure mode: in secure mode there is no fallback, so a DoH server
that is unreachable - a captive portal, a hotel network, a resolver having a bad
day - means no name resolution at all, with nothing in the UI explaining why.
Chromium picks automatic for that reason and so does this. Anyone who wants
secure mode can already have it by choosing a specific provider in Settings,
which is what that choice means; and with change 2 above it now keeps working
where it used to fall over. Getting the request's stated goal - reaching sites
a plaintext resolver blocks - does not require taking the browser's DNS down
with it when the DoH server is unreachable.

Also not taken: the rename of DohProviderEntry::ui_name to ui_name_cromite and
the forcing of display_globally, which exists to show every provider in the
picker regardless of country. Aerium already has its own provider list in
aerium-dns-providers.patch, so that is churn on a NET_EXPORT struct for
something already solved here. And two LOG lines Cromite uses for its own
debugging.


Verified against the real 152.0.7977.82 sources. All five files were fetched at
that tag and every anchor confirmed present before anything was written -
LOAD_FLAG(IS_MAIN_FRAME_ORIGIN_RECENTLY_ACCESSED, 1 << 19) is still the highest
bit in use, so 1 << 20 is free; DnsConfigOverrides really does declare
dns_over_https_config and secure_dns_mode as std::optional, so the added
condition compiles as written. The patch was then generated from the pair and
re-applied to a clean copy: it lands with no fuzz and reproduces all five files
byte for byte. Nothing else in the series edits any of them - the single grep
hit on stub_resolver_config_reader.cc is a comment in aerium-first-run-page.patch.
base/feature_list.h is already included in that file at line 13, and both call
site anchors match exactly once.

